WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.970
- Welcome back.

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:02.510
We've got a full couch y'all,

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:04.290
Tony Gonzalez, Josh Brolin,

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.000
and best selling author Sabaa Tahir.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.210
(audience applauding)

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.740
We've been talking about my
stranger Read's initiative

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.180
which promotes reading,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.270
something my next guest
is deeply passionate

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.880
about she's an education
activist, an advocate, an author

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.627
and in 2014, she became
the youngest recipient

00:00:20.627 --> 00:00:22.770
of the Nobel peace prize

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:27.770
putting my sports Emmy to shame,
dial dialed in from London,

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:29.987
Please welcome Malala Yousafzai.

00:00:29.987 --> 00:00:32.528
(audience applauding)

00:00:32.528 --> 00:00:33.361
- Hello?

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.240
- Okay So Malala, like,
what was the first book you

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.720
read that made you a lifelong reader?

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.800
- My favorite book has been the Alchemist.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.280
It was one of the first books
I read when I was a little kid

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.570
in Pakistan.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:50.730
And a lot of students were
limited to school textbooks

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:53.680
because they did not have enough
libraries or they were not

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.219
encouraged to read books
outside their school textbooks.

00:00:56.219 --> 00:00:59.170
So I was very lucky that
I was gifted this book.

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:02.140
And since then I have
become an avid reader

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.000
and I really am considering
myself fortunate to have access

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.470
to libraries and books
now and to be able to read

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:11.480
- That's amazing, like
reading something that cool

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.460
like so young.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.160
So you're, you're tireless
advocate for girls education.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:17.291
So what are some of the
biggest challenges that you

00:01:17.291 --> 00:01:19.290
you think we face right now?

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.110
- There are 130 million girls

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:25.960
out of school and the recent
events, including the pandemic

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.250
and climate related
changes is making it worse.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.850
More girls are at risk of
losing their education.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.180
And I think something that
really needs our attention

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.380
right now is what's
happening in Afghanistan.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.220
Afghanistan is the only country

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.900
in the world where adolescent
girls are forbidden

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.720
from having access to
educate it is unimaginable.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.130
Like why can't a girl go to school?

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.600
Why can't she have the right
to be able to read and write?

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.600
So it must wake us up and
challenge us to speak out.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.870
Women in Afghanistan are on the streets.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:01.160
They're protesting for
their right to have access

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.190
to education, to have
access to the right to work.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.227
So, you know, I know that
I receive so much support

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:09.733
around the world because
I was one of those girls.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.910
And I shared my story
and people heard my story

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:14.304
but I want to remind everyone

00:02:14.304 --> 00:02:17.100
that the story of Malala is not unique.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.845
It is a story of millions of girls

00:02:18.845 --> 00:02:21.645
who need our attention,
who need our support.

00:02:21.645 --> 00:02:22.478
(audience applauding)

00:02:22.478 --> 00:02:26.860
- Oh, that's, it's so sad that
there's so many out there.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:29.950
You, y'all have a connection

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.770
so you've never met Malala,
but you have met her dad?

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.330
- I'm so starstruck right now

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:34.900
and I don't ever get starstruck.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.900
And I so appreciate who you are.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.980
I did have a back and forth

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:42.160
with your father who wrote an introduction

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.080
for Steve McCrory's photographs.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.380
He just did a book and
he's a friend of mine.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.910
So I ended up actually having
an Instagram back and forth

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.180
with your dad. Yeah, and I loved it.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.200
There was any connection with you

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.014
cause I have three girls in
what you do means the world.

00:02:58.014 --> 00:03:00.764
(audience applauding)

00:03:00.764 --> 00:03:01.753
- It's such an honor.

00:03:01.753 --> 00:03:03.390
It's such

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:04.223
- Oh man.

00:03:04.223 --> 00:03:05.056
That's so cool.

00:03:05.056 --> 00:03:06.250
It was such a random
thing that we found out.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:07.240
We were like, how? What?

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.073
- I know.

00:03:08.073 --> 00:03:09.000
Yeah, the world is smaller than we think

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.052
- Me and her dad are hanging out.

00:03:10.052 --> 00:03:10.885
We're bros.

00:03:10.885 --> 00:03:11.718
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:11.718 --> 00:03:12.902
You are Instagram bros.

00:03:12.902 --> 00:03:15.360
So, Malala, you've also
found a new way to advocate

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.610
for reading and education.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.220
So can you tell us about it?

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.140
- Yes, I'm part of Literati

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:23.170
and I am their chief education advisor

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.360
and we know that not
all children have access

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.450
to books and resources of education

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.770
and this pandemic has made
it worse for many children.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.890
So I'm working with
Literati as this advisor

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:39.120
and I am here to ensure that children

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.250
especially in underserved
communities can have access

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:43.848
to books that inspire them.

00:03:43.848 --> 00:03:46.530
So Literati has made this commitment to

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:49.760
donate 100,000 books to those kids.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.440
And I hope that, you know

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:55.100
more kids can get inspired
to believe in themselves

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:58.209
and step up for changing their communities

00:03:58.209 --> 00:04:02.075
and following the dreams
that they believe in.

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:02.908
(audience applauding)

00:04:02.908 --> 00:04:04.377
- Absolutely, that's so cool.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.460
Do y'all remember a book that
just completely changed you?

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.210
kind of opened your
eyes, mine was Matilda.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.750
I just been like the fourth grade.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.120
I thought it was magical
and it made me love reading.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.600
So was there anything like that for y'all?

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.840
- I had one, I had one.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.990
It was called Seven
Daughters and Seven Sons.

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:21.490
It was by Barbara Cohen
and Bahija Lovejoy.

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:24.440
I read it in fourth grade
and I checked it out so much

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.490
that at the end of the year
I was like, can I buy this?

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.341
And my librarian was
like, you could lose it.

00:04:29.341 --> 00:04:30.174
(audience laughing)

00:04:30.174 --> 00:04:31.007
- Oh, I like her.

00:04:31.007 --> 00:04:32.300
- You're so sweet.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.630
And it was the first time I saw

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:35.463
- It's Damaged.

00:04:35.463 --> 00:04:37.990
- I know it's like, we
don't know where it went.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.470
It was, it was the first
time I had seen Arabic words

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:42.303
in a book.

00:04:42.303 --> 00:04:44.950
The first time I saw a
brown girl as like the hero

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.060
and it made me know that I
was here, that we were here

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:49.893
we were present.

00:04:49.893 --> 00:04:50.726
We, we were,

00:04:50.726 --> 00:04:51.559
- we have stories,

00:04:51.559 --> 00:04:53.730
- we have stories and that
they are worth sharing.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:55.590
And so it was such a special book for me

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:57.770
and I, I still, you know,
I still have, that's

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:58.913
- It totally made an impact
on the trajectory of your life

00:04:58.913 --> 00:05:00.370
- Trajectory of my life.

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:01.330
Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.610
And I still keep it in a
place of honor on my shelf.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:04.443
And like, it's just such

00:05:04.443 --> 00:05:05.760
- In a place of honor.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:06.670
I'm stealing that.

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:07.503
- Yeah,

00:05:07.503 --> 00:05:08.511
- People are gonna come over

00:05:08.511 --> 00:05:09.560
and I'm be like, it's in a
place of honor in my shelf.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.740
- Exactly, yes, high up on the shelf.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:13.080
- I'm stealing it.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.374
Am gonna say I made it up.
I'm just kidding.

00:05:15.374 --> 00:05:16.207
- Totally fine.

00:05:16.207 --> 00:05:17.040
I'll give you credit.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.990
Did y'all have a book like that?

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:22.840
- Yeah, actually I'm a little
bit later to the game reading

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:24.960
which I think is hopefully
people out there go look

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.070
it's never too late.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:30.890
But my going into my second,
third year in the NFL

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:32.472
my second year in the NFL, I dropped

00:05:32.472 --> 00:05:34.890
I led the NFL and drop passes.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.190
And so I got benched twice.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:38.840
And my brother sent me this book

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.880
by Vince Lombardi called
when pride mattered.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.610
And it was the first book that ever read

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.940
from beginning to the end
that wasn't required reading.

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.770
And the whole, I don't know
if most people don't know

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:49.140
who Vince Lombardi is or if you do,

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.670
he is one of the greatest
coaches of all time in the NFL.

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:54.110
It's what the super bowl
trophies named after.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:55.340
- No, I knew cause I have sports Emmy.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:56.360
- You got the sports Emmy.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.230
So, so you know, you know that

00:05:58.230 --> 00:05:59.290
- I have knowledge that I can drop

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:00.390
like that all the time.

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:01.223
Yeah.

00:06:01.223 --> 00:06:03.760
- But, but that book, that
book changed my life where

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.380
I read that book and I got so excited.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.740
Like the greatest moment
in a man's life is when

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.420
he lies totally exhausted
on the battlefield glorious.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:12.230
And off of that, I went
out and I started reading

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.267
of all these other books by coaches.

00:06:14.267 --> 00:06:15.100
- I love that

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.240
- Biographies and then since then,

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.160
I mean, I'm an avid reader.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:17.993
I don't ever

00:06:17.993 --> 00:06:18.930
- It's like once you find
something that really

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:20.900
peaks your interest,
then it just takes off.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.340
- Yeah, and you see what,
what reading can do, how

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.940
it can change your life and
the people's lives around you.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:27.470
It's such a domino effect.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:28.490
It's not just you.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:30.030
That's learning, it's you learning

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:31.930
and then spreading that
information to everybody else.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:32.763
It's a cool thing.

00:06:32.763 --> 00:06:33.596
(audience applauding)

00:06:33.596 --> 00:06:34.429
- Absolutely, absolutely.

00:06:34.429 --> 00:06:36.090
And expanding your kids' vocabulary like

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.416
with children that's why
we read all the time.

00:06:38.416 --> 00:06:39.249
Did you have one?

00:06:39.249 --> 00:06:41.570
- Just reading is a huge
thing in my household

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:44.050
and it always has been, but
I , you know, growing up

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:45.890
on a ranch, I think I just
said it to you growing up

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:47.720
on a ranch you're out in
the middle of nowhere.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:49.518
You're feeding 65 horses every day.

00:06:49.518 --> 00:06:54.518
And then I read Ray Bradbury's
Fahrenheit 451, which is

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.060
about censorship and all that ironically.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.410
And just exploded at my brain.

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:01.580
I mean, I was just, you
see, through the hue

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:03.725
of what you're reading
instead of having to deal

00:07:03.725 --> 00:07:06.640
with the shower reality of
what you're dealing with

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:08.820
which is, oh, I'm doing
this thing every day

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.280
and it's monotonous and I'm
out in the middle of nowhere

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.500
and then suddenly
there's color everywhere.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.958
And just reading is everything.

00:07:14.958 --> 00:07:15.791
- That's wonderful.
It's color everywhere.

00:07:15.791 --> 00:07:17.966
I'm stealing that too.

00:07:17.966 --> 00:07:19.930
I'm a club though.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:22.401
So as you know, our stranger
reads initiative is all

00:07:22.401 --> 00:07:24.120
about sharing books with strangers.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:26.110
So Malala, I was curious

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.620
if you wanted to share a book with me

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:30.280
and I would share one of my
favorites with you today.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.150
- Of course, I would love
to share a book with you.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.904
Last year my Literati book club

00:07:33.904 --> 00:07:36.970
had this memoir called Aftershocks

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:40.560
by debut author, Nadia Owusu.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:43.040
And she is abandoned by her mother

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.230
at age two and orphaned by her you know,

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:47.850
when her father dies at age 13 and this is

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:50.740
about how this young woman
has to create her own home

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:52.680
and her own place in the world

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.000
and the strength and
courage it takes to do that.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.300
And I think it would really resonate

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.120
with you and all your viewers, Kelly.

00:07:58.120 --> 00:07:59.638
So I hope you would enjoy that.

00:07:59.638 --> 00:08:00.471
(audience applauding)

00:08:00.471 --> 00:08:01.330
- Oh, I love it.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.580
And I have not read it.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:05.436
So I'm very excited about it.

00:08:05.436 --> 00:08:06.370
I have.

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:07.850
Okay, I love this book.

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:11.940
I'm about to show you, it
literally sent me on such a path.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.258
It's called the language of flowers

00:08:14.258 --> 00:08:18.130
by Vanessa Diffenbauch,
and it's so, so good.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.610
The story is kind of similar

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:21.770
in the sense that the, the
female character, the lead

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:24.680
in it has to, like, she goes
through like 30 foster homes.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:25.513
It's just like

00:08:25.513 --> 00:08:27.100
kind of just keeps
hitting hurdles, you know

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:28.270
hurdle after hurdle.

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:30.629
And, and it just kind of
tells her this she's very

00:08:30.629 --> 00:08:35.360
much introverted and she
starts to speak via flowers.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:37.030
Like she makes these
incredible and they all

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:38.050
every flower has a meaning

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:39.900
and it kind of goes
back to Victorian times,

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:40.733
Which I didn't know.

00:08:40.733 --> 00:08:41.820
And that's how they communicated

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:44.120
especially lovers, with flower.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:45.490
I know exciting now,

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:48.970
it got Bridgerton y'all,(audience
laughing) but no, it's

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:50.520
it's the language of flowers and it's

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:51.530
and it's just an amazing thing

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:52.930
how she ends up communicating it.

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:54.600
She finds it, it's her like
kind of vessel of communicating.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.760
And then it ends up kind of opening her

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.530
up to relationships and
like different, you know

00:08:58.530 --> 00:08:59.420
things that she wouldn't have done.

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:00.471
But it's so interesting.

00:09:00.471 --> 00:09:02.100
And I had no idea that there was

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.826
like a language with
flowers and now I'm obsessed

00:09:04.826 --> 00:09:06.467
and I send the case to everyone

00:09:06.467 --> 00:09:07.610
(audience laughing)

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:09.060
and they all have meaning.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:10.780
It's like, it's very,
it's very, very cool.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:12.730
And it was just a different kind of novel

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:15.118
that I'd never read. So
anyway, I hope you like it.

00:09:15.118 --> 00:09:16.878
(audience applauding)

00:09:16.878 --> 00:09:18.438
- I'd love to read it.

00:09:18.438 --> 00:09:22.030
And for me, the high of this
show is meeting all of you.

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:24.700
And also I love your books, Kelly.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:25.720
I love them.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:27.031
- Oh, thank you.
Thank you so much.

00:09:27.031 --> 00:09:28.468
(audience applauding)

00:09:28.468 --> 00:09:29.368
- Thank everybody.

